# Timing-independent firmware/ corpus for the P3.4 lockstep harness --
# per the plan, the subset of firmware/Makefile targets whose outcome
# doesn't depend on interrupt/JTAG/debugger timing (excludes
# interrupt_test, uart_plic_meip_test, uart_plic_seip_test,
# m10_debug_test). One `make -C firmware` TARGET name per line (not a
# path -- run_lockstep.sh builds each before running it).
#
# These programs halt via a trailing ebreak (crt0.s's own convention,
# checked externally by each program's own dedicated _tb.sv in the
# regular regression suite) rather than writing a tohost location, so
# they're unusable with lockstep_tb.sv's +TOHOST_ADDR completion path --
# run_lockstep.sh instead runs each with +MAX_RETIRE=<bound> (comparing
# every retirement up to that bound, including the harmless repeated
# retirements of the post-ebreak halt loop). #-comments and blank lines
# ignored.
#
# target | hex file (relative to firmware/) | MAX_RETIRE bound
#
# KNOWN GAP, not yet resolved: `all` (crt0.hex, hello.c) is excluded --
# a run against it produces no output at all after the very first
# +TIMEOUT=%0d/+MAX_RETIRE heartbeat (confirmed via a fine-grained
# heartbeat print: exactly one line at cycle=0, both sides' PC still 0,
# then silence forever, killed cleanly by the wall-clock timeout with no
# error). Every other firmware entry below, and the whole 111-entry ACT4
# corpus, complete normally, so this looks specific to this one hex
# image rather than a general MAX_RETIRE-completion bug -- possibly
# related to firmware/wb4_sram.sv's own auto-load also targeting this
# exact file (`../firmware/crt0.hex`), untested elsewhere since every
# other corpus entry uses a different image than that auto-load default.
# Next step if picked back up: the same fine-grained-heartbeat technique
# that root-caused lockstep_tb.sv's/lockstep_wb_delay.sv's own earlier
# bugs, but here the very first heartbeat already looks stuck, so also
# check init-block/readmemh ordering (see wb4_sram.sv's own header
# comment on the required `#1` sequencing before this run start.
# all|crt0.hex|2000
csr_test|csr_test.hex|3000
m_test|m_test.hex|3000
priv_test|priv_test.hex|4000
a_test|a_test.hex|3000
c_test|c_test.hex|3000
sv39_real_test|sv39_real_test.hex|6000
